<Base
  title="Two Rust photo apps in the browser — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="LightCraft is another wonderful Lightroom replacement written in Rust that runs in a browser tab. Here is what I learned when I put it next to LightPhotos. I include some measurements, but they are not a fully equal comparison."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-07">October 7, 2026</time> &middot; updated <time datetime="2026-10-09">October 9</time></p>
        <h1>Two Rust photo apps in the browser</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          <a href="https://github.com/storytold/lightcraft">LightCraft</a>
          is another wonderful Lightroom replacement, written in Rust, that
          runs in a browser tab. Here is what I learned when I put it next to
          LightPhotos. I include some measurements, but they are not a fully
          equal comparison.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 9 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          Both apps are Rust and egui compiled to WebAssembly, with downloads
          of 3.6&nbsp;MB and 3.8&nbsp;MB compressed. Updated October 9. On
          twenty Sony RAW files, LightPhotos filled its grid in 1.2&nbsp;s
          and showed each next photo in 0.10&nbsp;s. LightCraft took
          2.5&nbsp;s and 0.97&nbsp;s, and used about 40% more memory. Both
          apps develop the RAW data at fit-to-window. The difference is when:
          LightPhotos now develops the photos on either side while you look
          at the current one, so the arrow key finds the work already done.
          LightCraft develops each photo when you arrive.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="start-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-act="next" aria-pressed="true">Next photo</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-act="jump" aria-pressed="false">Jump in the grid</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 232" role="img" aria-label="What each app starts from. A RAW file holds a JPEG that the camera made, and the sensor data. For the next photo, LightPhotos already developed the sensor data at the window's size while you looked at the photo before, and shows it in 0.10 seconds. LightCraft develops the sensor data when you arrive and shows the photo in 0.97 seconds. After a jump, LightPhotos first shows the camera's JPEG, then its develop.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">ONE RAW FILE</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="250" y="12">WORK FOR THE NEXT PHOTO</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="26" width="200" height="180" rx="8"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" data-el="jpeg" x="16" y="40" width="168" height="64" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="32" y="66">Embedded JPEG</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="32" y="86">made by the camera</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="16" y="128" width="168" height="64" rx="6"></rect>
